          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Cordelia’s loving state toward Lear. Its qualification by her filial bond limits the degree or kind of love claimed but does not diminish its clear truth-presenting form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not itself undertake a new duty or promise future conduct. The adjoining reference to her “bond” invokes an existing filial obligation and defines the measure of her love; it should not be transferred onto the love avowal as a separate commitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"This is a controlled answer to Lear’s demand, framed as a measured avowal rather than a reflex-like or affectively discharged cry.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The focal expression’s core force is adequately captured as an avowal of love. Its respectful form of address and contextual contrast with her sisters do not establish an additional core mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks Cordelia to declare how much she loves him in order to obtain a richer portion of the kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” she gives this restrained avowal, explicitly measuring her love by her filial bond rather than competing in unlimited praise.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target makes no new undertaking. In the surrounding speech Cordelia invokes existing filial duties—“Obey you, love you, and most honour you”—but that is an account of duties she returns, not a promise contained in the focal expression. Lear does not accept her answer as satisfactory; he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene supports the represented sincerity of Cordelia’s affection through her asides and explanations, but it cannot independently settle every fact about her inner state or later conduct. No omitted context is assumed.\"}"
